    pr_ref:  # 9a10
    description: "The OTP linker emits two edge_type values -- otp_call and otp_cast -- that are NOT in the canonical edge_type registry (edge_types.py), yet the static L3 producer-coherence gate (test_no_strict_producer_violations_for_edge_type) stays GREEN. Mechanic: the values are carried via dict-indirection -- CALL_TYPE_MAP {'call': ('.handle_call','otp_call'), 'cast': ('.handle_cast','otp_cast')} at linkers/otp.py:62-63 and ERLANG_CALL_TYPE_MAP at :99-100 -- then tuple-unpacked into Edge.create at otp.py:397/491. The static walker (find_emitted_edge_types, which scans Edge.create(edge_type=literal)) cannot resolve the value through the dict subscript + tuple unpack, so it never sees otp_call/otp_cast and the gate reports clean. Exposure: when Erlang/Elixir OTP code is analyzed, 2 unregistered edge_type values ship; any consumer enumerating edge types from the registry misses them. Two sub-questions: (a) gate blind-spot -- extend the producer-coherence walker to resolve dict-indirection/tuple-unpack emit sites, or add a runtime-corpus check; (b) vocabulary -- register-as-canonical vs fold to dispatches_to/calls + meta['mechanism'] (no consumer branches on these today). Confirmed read-only on dev d9bdc39d0a. NOTE: a scope sweep also claimed SCIP has_type/defined_by have the same shape, but I could NOT reproduce those (no such edge_type literals found) -- verify separately."  # 9a10
    fields: {}  # 9a10
